# GitHub uses the LAST pattern that matches, not the most specific one.
#
# apex/, validations/ and services/ sit at the same level and none is inside
# another, so no pattern below overlaps another pattern and the order of these
# lines cannot change who approves a path. The only ordering rule is that the
# catch-all comes first.
#
# This file is a document, not a control, unless the default branch requires a
# review from code owners, dismisses stale approvals on a push, and makes no
# exception for administrators. See docs/branch-protection.md.
#
# Every line names the same account, because that is the truth of a one-person
# repository -- GitHub will not let anyone approve their own pull request, so
# requiring code-owner review here would lock the only owner out. The three
# tiers below are kept as structure: they are what an organisation maps to
# @org/dns-critical-approvers, @org/dns-service-approvers and each owning team,
# and turning on `require_code_owner_reviews` is the first thing to do when a
# second person arrives.

*                                          @matt-FFFFFF

/terraform/                                @matt-FFFFFF
/policy/                                   @matt-FFFFFF
/tools/                                    @matt-FFFFFF

# The pinned toolchain. Named rather than left to the catch-all: changing the
# Terraform version here changes what applies to production DNS.
/mise.toml                                 @matt-FFFFFF

# Critical: the zone itself, everything that answers at the apex, and the files
# that decide who may approve anything.
/zones/*/zone.yaml                         @matt-FFFFFF
/zones/*/apex/                             @matt-FFFFFF
/.github/                                  @matt-FFFFFF

# Validation records, and the life cycle of a service.
/zones/*/validations/                      @matt-FFFFFF
/zones/*/services/*/service.yaml           @matt-FFFFFF

# Team self-service. Add a line when a service is created; remove it when the
# service is removed or delegated away.
#
# Note what this produces. Creating a service needs a critical approver for this
# one line and a service approver for service.yaml, because the file that grants
# merge rights is itself owned by the critical approvers. Changing or removing a
# service that already exists needs only a service approver. That is a
# consequence of the structure and not an extra rule: you cannot hand out merge
# rights without editing the file that hands out merge rights.
#
# If the service approvers alone should be able to create a service, delete
# these lines and enforce service.yaml's `owner` with a required status check
# instead, trading GitHub's review logic for CI.
/zones/*/services/home/records.yaml        @matt-FFFFFF
